00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:04,575
Teacher midterm practice for C plus plus, question 28.

2
00:00:05,195 --> 00:00:09,238
Question two evaluates y equals cosine of x for x from zero to

3
00:00:09,238 --> 00:00:13,867
one thousand, in steps of one times ten to the minus three, and

4
00:00:13,867 --> 00:00:17,975
counts how many times y is greater than zero point nine.

5
00:00:17,975 --> 00:00:21,757
Which loop header gives exactly the right x values?

6
00:00:23,457 --> 00:00:27,865
Drive the loop with an integer counter, and compute x from it: i from

7
00:00:27,865 --> 00:00:32,215
zero to one million, and x equals i times ten to the minus three.

8
00:00:32,835 --> 00:00:37,639
That covers zero to one thousand inclusive, with no accumulated round off.

9
00:00:37,639 --> 00:00:41,087
Running it counts one hundred forty three thousand, eight

10
00:00:41,087 --> 00:00:44,365
hundred seventy eight values above zero point nine.

11
00:00:44,985 --> 00:00:46,721
The other headers fail.

12
00:00:46,721 --> 00:00:51,466
Not equal to one thousand may never become false after a million additions.

13
00:00:51,466 --> 00:00:55,342
i less than one thousand stops at zero point nine nine nine.

14
00:00:55,342 --> 00:00:58,872
And x plus plus steps by one, not by a thousandth.

15
00:01:00,473 --> 00:01:04,090
So the right header is a for loop with int i from zero to one

16
00:01:04,090 --> 00:01:08,003
million, and x equal to i times ten to the minus three.

17
00:01:08,822 --> 00:01:13,394
Ending a floating point loop with not equals, instead of using an integer counter.

18
00:01:14,114 --> 00:01:19,568
Exam tip: work out the number of steps first, the range divided by the step size.

19
00:01:19,568 --> 00:01:22,863
Then loop an integer over exactly that many values.
